## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to frame a sign-assignment problem as a DP and implement both memoization and tabulation, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP state reduction to subset sum
- Space optimization with a 1D array
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tabulation and Memo versions
function countTargetSumTab(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const dp = Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const n of nums) {
    for (let t = target; t >= n; t--) dp[t] += dp[t-n];
  }
  return dp[target];
}

function countTargetSumMemo(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, sum){
    if (sum > target) return 0;
    if (i === nums.length) return sum === target ? 1 : 0;
    const key = i+':'+sum;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i+1, sum+nums[i]) + dfs(i+1, sum);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0,0);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of handling duplicate-heavy inputs efficiently and the ability to implement in-place sorting algorithms with optimized space usage.

## Key Concepts
- **3-way partitioning**: Separates elements into three groups: less than, equal to, and greater than the pivot
- **In-place quicksort**: Achieves O(1) auxiliary space (excluding recursion stack)
- **Pivot selection**: Random sampling avoids worst-case behavior on sorted inputs; median-of-three provides more robust estimates
- **Complexity analysis**: Best/average O(n log n); worst O(n²) when pivot consistently produces imbalanced partitions

## Code Example
```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  if (lo >= hi) return;
  const pivot = a[Math.floor(Math.random() * (hi - lo + 1)) + lo];
  let lt = lo, i = lo, gt = hi;
  while (i <= gt) {
    if (a[i] < pivot) {
      [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]];
      lt++; i++;
    } else if (a[i] > pivot) {
      [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]];
      gt--;
    } else {
      i++;
    }
  }
  threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
  threeWayQuicksort(a, gt + 1, hi);
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Best case**: O(n log n) — partitions remain balanced
- **Average case**: O(n log n) — expected with random pivot
- **Worst case**: O(n²) — occurs with sorted input and poor pivot (e.g., always picking first or last element)

## Pivot Selection Trade-offs
| Strategy | Pros | Cons |
|----------|------|------|
| Random | Simple, avoids adversarial input | Still possible to pick poor pivots |
| Median-of-three | More robust estimates | Slightly more computation |

The 3-way partition is particularly valuable for arrays with many duplicates, as it reduces unnecessary swaps and comparisons for elements equal to the pivot.

## Why This Is Asked
This problem validates DP mastery (two implementations) and handling of substring lookups with a dictionary. It stresses practical constraints and edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- Bottom-up DP, Memoization, Time: O(N * maxLen)
- Hash-map lookups for dictionary scores
- Edge cases: no decomposition, empty input, overlapping choices

## Code Example
```javascript
// Bottom-up
function maxScore(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const n = s.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(NEG);
  dp[0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(i, maxLen); len++) {
      const substr = s.slice(i - len, i);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr) && dp[i - len] !== NEG) {
        dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[i - len] + dict[substr]);
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n] === NEG ? -1 : dp[n];
}

// Top-down
function maxScoreTopDown(s, dict) {
  const maxLen = Math.max(...Object.keys(dict).map(k => k.length));
  const memo = new Map();
  function solve(i) {
    if (i === s.length) return 0;
    if (memo.has(i)) return memo.get(i);
    let best = -Infinity;
    for (let len = 1; len <= Math.min(maxLen, s.length - i); len++) {
      const substr = s.substring(i, i + len);
      if (dict.hasOwnProperty(substr)) {
        const val = dict[substr] + solve(i + len);
        if (val > best) best = val;
      }
    }
    memo.set(i, best);
    return best;
  }
  const res = solve(0);
  return res === -Infinity ? -1 : res;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt it if dictionary lookups are expensive and you need pruning?
- How would you extend to handle negative scores or required minimum total?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP with dual objectives: minimize cost and count optimal deletion sets, plus implement in two styles. It also covers edge cases like empty substrings and ties.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings; store (cost, ways).
- Memoization and bottom-up parallels; careful counting on ties.
- Modulo arithmetic for counts.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Memoized DFS
function solveMemo(s, delCost){
  const n = s.length;
  const memoCost = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(-1));
  const memoWays = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  function dfs(l,r){
    if (l>=r) return {cost:0, ways:1};
    if (memoCost[l][r]!==-1) return {cost:memoCost[l][r], ways:memoWays[l][r]};
    if (s[l]===s[r]){
      const res = dfs(l+1,r-1);
      memoCost[l][r]=res.cost;
      memoWays[l][r]=res.ways;
      return res;
    } else {
      const A = dfs(l+1,r);
      const B = dfs(l,r-1);
      let cost;
      let ways = 0;
      if (A.cost + delCost[l] < B.cost + delCost[r]){
        cost = A.cost + delCost[l];
        ways = A.ways;
      } else if (B.cost + delCost[r] < A.cost + delCost[l]){
        cost = B.cost + delCost[r];
        ways = B.ways;
      } else {
        cost = A.cost + Math.min(delCost[l], delCost[r]);
        ways = (A.ways + B.ways) % MOD;
      }
      memoCost[l][r]=cost; memoWays[l][r]=ways;
      return {cost, ways};
    }
  }
  return dfs(0,n-1);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of partition DP with sliding window optimization and modular arithmetic, plus dual implementation styles.

## Key Concepts

- Contiguous partitions
- Sliding window to enforce per-session bound
- Prefix-sum style accumulation for O(n) time

## Code Example

```javascript
function bottomUpCount(a, B) {
  const n = a.length, MOD = 1000000007;
  const dp = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  let left = 0, window = 0, acc = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    window += a[i-1];
    while (window > B) {
      window -= a[left];
      acc = (acc - dp[left] + MOD) % MOD;
      left++;
    }
    acc = (acc + dp[i-1]) % MOD;
    dp[i] = acc;
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

